> DarthBob88 wrote:
>> A couple of questions for y'all.
>> 1 Firstly, how does one apply one of these patches on a Linux system?
>> Go into the nethack directory, then do "patch <(Patchfile)"?
>
> Basically yes.  You need to make sure you have the source code not just
> the binary.  Once you apply the patch you need to recompile and
> reinstall.

To be a bit more precise, what I recommand is:
_ get the sources.
_ configure, compile and install a non-patched version. This may take some  
times and you certainly want to sort out compilation problems *before*  
going into patching problems...
_ once you have that, save the official sources with working config files  
(eg, rename the directory to something like  
/usr/srg/nethack-3.4.3-official).
_ make a separate copy for patching (eg  
/usr/src/nethack-3.4.3-<patch-name>). This will allow to keep a clean and  
working source if you ever need to redo things from start (patching error,  
...)
_ then, simply going into the source dir and "patch -pn < patch". Get a  
look at the early lines of the patch file, it should look like that:
    diff -pruN slashem-0.0.7E7F1-official-release/include/mondata.h  
slashem-0.0.7E7F1-gold-dragon/include/mondata.h
(taken from my port to Slash'em of Nephi (?)'s gold dragon patch)

you see here that the patch suppose that the unpatched stuff was in  
slashem-(...)-official and the patched stuff in slashem-(...)-gold-dragon.  
Of course, the exact directories names might not be the same on your  
computer and in the patch... hence you have to tell patch that the  
beginning of the path is irrelevant. This is done by the "-pn" option  
where n is the number of '/' you want to ignore (at the beginning of the  
path). In the case of the example above, that would be 1.

So, to sum up:
_ try compiling first.
_ keep a working copy of the unpatched sources.
_ "patch -pn < <patch-file>"



